hi i'm bimarunmi i'm emma daly yakima  and this ramadan i'm going to be making  some nigerian style potato and egg for  sahur and i'm going to be making some  pakistani style pakoras for iftar  ramadan is a month of the islamic  calendar and during that month you fast  from sun up to sundown every day and so  sahora is the pre-dawn meal you have  this before the sun comes up and when i  was growing up the thing that we had  most commonly was something that we  nigerians call egg singular always egg  you can put anything in there like  sardines or corned beef or sausages or  whatever like it really is a diy  situation i think like eggs are like a  really common thing they're easy to  quickly high protein super nutritious  and they fill you up for longer so you  don't feel the pangs of hunger nearly as  much you're looking at basically like 12  to 19 hours of not eating or drinking  during the day and that includes water  before you ask fasting means different  things to different people but it's  ultimately a way to try to make yourself  a better person during the month of  ramadan unfortunately also what happens  is sometimes when you break your fast  you're very hungry and you eat a lot of  food so the thing that i make is  something that i like shovel in my face  when it's time to break fast tell us a  little bit about what you're going to  make for iftar so iftar is the time  where you break your fast you know you  hear the call to prayer you eat a date  and you drink some water and then  somebody's probably already preparing  the food and one of the things that we  always prepare is this bakora which is a  fried vegetable fritter the pakoras can  basically be any fried vegetable it's  like the chickpea flour is the thing  that really makes it what it is usually  potatoes onions jalapenos eggplant it's  really anything you want so it's like a  really  special treat this is like the unhealthy  side of your fast breaking um what i  love about pakoras is that um they're  not always pretty they're just like a  whole bunch of massive vegetables and  batter on top of them so i like it when  there's like a lot like a jalapeno and  an onion and a potato in one pakora they  are like a really special thing of  bringing people together where everyone  wants to pluck them right out of the oil  when they're hot they don't even get  into a plate really you know because  they're best when they're immediately  crispy out of the oil  [Music]  i love ramadan every year it makes me  feel really connected like there are  literally millions of us across the  world all doing the same thing at the  same time i find that really  heartwarming and it makes me incredibly  happy like i'm really part of a global  community  yes  [Music]
 

